[[:db/cas 17592186281658 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=1,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=6.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-4,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =1+3+6 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4-6+18 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=1,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=6.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-4,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =1+3+6 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4-6+18 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281659 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=6,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =6+2+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-18+4+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{2}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=6,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =6+2+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-18+4+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{2}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281660 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=4,\\,{y}_{2}=2,\\,{y}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8+6+2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{16}{6}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=4,\\,{y}_{2}=2,\\,{y}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8+6+2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{16}{6}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281661 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=3,\\,{m}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=3,\\,{y}_{2}=5,\\,{y}_{3}=2,\\,{y}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+2+3+1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3}+{m}_{4}{y}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6+10+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{26}{8}=\\frac{13}{4}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=3,\\,{m}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=3,\\,{y}_{2}=5,\\,{y}_{3}=2,\\,{y}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+2+3+1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3}+{m}_{4}{y}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6+10+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{26}{8}=\\frac{13}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281664 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/a0/a042919f73d668cac20ead2752c689e57880f60bc096747f41cec3aeb3ed62ca.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/a0/a042919f73d668cac20ead2752c689e57880f60bc096747f41cec3aeb3ed62ca.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281665 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b6/b6e8af58b8e7ba5f909d226b895bb39b3fc94e53686e4718eb32c9bb4ee61c08.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot [y]_{y=0}^{y={x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b6/b6e8af58b8e7ba5f909d226b895bb39b3fc94e53686e4718eb32c9bb4ee61c08.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot [y]_{y=0}^{y={x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281666 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/30/3097e540292efd601743ba8de24acffc3b51469e77561b2bccbd799d1125e1ed.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14{y}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{y}^{4}}{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$" "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/30/3097e540292efd601743ba8de24acffc3b51469e77561b2bccbd799d1125e1ed.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14{y}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{y}^{4}}{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284388 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}$" "Find ${f}^{″}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284389 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,0).$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186284389 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+6x-4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4{y}^{3}-4x+8y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=6x+6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=12{y}^{2}+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x+6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12{y}^{2}+8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=12\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=80.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+6x-4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4{y}^{3}-4x+8y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=6x+6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=12{y}^{2}+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x+6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12{y}^{2}+8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=12\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=80.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284390 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186284390 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284391 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\pi ).$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\pi ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186284391 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,\\pi ),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\pi  & \\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,\\pi )=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-\\pi \\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,\\pi ),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\pi  & \\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,\\pi )=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-\\pi \\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284415 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4be9362c014a7b8f0d0708ca5d7ea32a1b5bb13d48e2e1704341d04b89f9566a.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}V=$" "![](images/4b/4be9362c014a7b8f0d0708ca5d7ea32a1b5bb13d48e2e1704341d04b89f9566a.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284454 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2}=1?$" "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284457 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?" "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.\n$$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"] [:db/cas 17592186284458 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2