t $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$"] [:db/cas 17592186284540 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).$" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284541 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $$\n\\displaystyle (x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284542 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284544 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284544 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284545 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284545 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284546 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x}\\,i+\\frac{z}{y}\\,j+k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x}\\,i+\\frac{z}{y}\\,j+k\n$$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$"] [:db/cas 17592186284547 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\,i+\\sqrt{y}\\,j+z\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\,i+\\sqrt{y}\\,j+z\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,2].$"] [:db/cas 17592186284550 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.$" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284551 :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes."] [:db/cas 17592186284589 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284590 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$"] [:db/cas 17592186284593 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186284601 :question/problem "What is the linear transformation $T$ whose standard matrix is \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What is the linear transformation $T$ whose standard matrix is \n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284612 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=6-2y$” is false, while statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” is true. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}.$\n\n![](images/48/487b57182e190cbacf3b1ad30f55d227b59fb470e8b5609ad72327d229e53e45.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” only." "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=6-2y$” is false, while statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” is true. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}.$\n\n![](images/48/487b57182e190cbacf3b1ad30f55d227b59fb470e8b5609ad72327d229e53e45.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186284623 :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$\\int \\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284627 :question/problem "For the curve $y=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." "For the curve $$\n\\displaystyle y=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},\n$$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186284627 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$" "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle y=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186284628 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$" "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284629 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ hence $c=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4} & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{5}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4}.$" "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ hence $c=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4} & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{5}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284633 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{{(2-x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{{(2-x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284654 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2}?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284654 :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a6/a67681a546ecd949fd8cc3be1d5d0647a3cbf88c8809bdc2c620f0d42cb89c8b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2},$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7dbfba1ee7e66fbc15858f668aacc27db4bdeebb3a7e28f243f2dc62216dda57.png)" "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a6/a67681a546ecd949fd8cc3be1d5d0647a3cbf88c8809bdc2c620f0d42cb89c8b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2},\n$$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7dbfba1ee7e66fbc15858f668aacc27db4bdeebb3a7e28f243f2dc62216dda57.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186284660 :question/problem "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186284661 :question/problem "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186284675 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{({x}^{3}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{({x}^{3}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284676 :question/problem "Evaluate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2-{x}^{3})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2-{x}^{3})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284678 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}).$" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284685 :question/problem "Express $z=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186284685 :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/