{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(2{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287708 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{3x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x+1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{3x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x+1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287714 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287821 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Given that the determinant of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186287824 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(A)=-30,$ find the value of $k.$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-30,$ find the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186287878 :question/problem "Let ${X}_{i}={0,i,2i},$ then ${⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=$" "Let ${X}_{i}={0,i,2i},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287879 :question/problem "Let ${S}_{i}=[0+\\frac{1}{i},1],$ then ${⋃}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=$" "Let ${S}_{i}=[0+\\frac{1}{i},1],$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287882 :question/problem "Let ${Y}_{i}=[-i,0],$ then ${⋃}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{Y}_{i}=$" "Let ${Y}_{i}=[-i,0],$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{Y}_{i}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287883 :question/problem "Let ${S}_{i}=[1+\\frac{1}{i},2],$ then ${⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=$" "Let ${S}_{i}=[1+\\frac{1}{i},2],$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287883 :question/worked-solution "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }$ means that our set of indexes is \n${5,6,7…}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=5,$ we have ${S}_{5}=[1+\\frac{1}{5},2]=[\\frac{6}{5},2]$\n\n- if $i=6,$ we have ${S}_{6}=[1+\\frac{1}{6},2]=[\\frac{7}{6},2]$\n\n- if $i=7,$ we have ${S}_{7}=[1+\\frac{1}{7},2]=[\\frac{8}{7},2]$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals approaches $1$ but never reaches it, and\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals equals $2$ for all the sets.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=(1,2].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }$ means that our set of indexes is \n${5,6,7…}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=5,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{5}=[1+\\frac{1}{5},2]=[\\frac{6}{5},2]\n$$\n\n- if $i=6,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{6}=[1+\\frac{1}{6},2]=[\\frac{7}{6},2]\n$$\n\n- if $i=7,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{7}=[1+\\frac{1}{7},2]=[\\frac{8}{7},2]\n$$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals approaches $1$ but never reaches it, and\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals equals $2$ for all the sets.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=(1,2].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287927 :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287944 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287945 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287946 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287952 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{3+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{3+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287978 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}(3z+2)\\,\\text{d}z.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}(3z+2)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287987 :question/problem "$\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186287996 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288000 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288006 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288010 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” and “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” are true." "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186288011 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288013 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186288016 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290265 :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290275 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290276 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290277 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290281 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290291 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int {x}^{2}{4}^{{x}^{3}/5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}{4}^{{x}^{3}/5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290333 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=x+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=x+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290336 :question/problem "$\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290340 :question/problem "$\\int (4csc⁡xcot⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4csc⁡xcot⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290366 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290380 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186290380 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =4(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1)^{3}(-1-2)=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1)^{3}(1-2)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =(3)^{3}(3-2)=27>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$” only." "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =4(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1)^{3}(-1-2)=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1)^{3}(1-2)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =(3)^{3}(3-2)=27>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186290382 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186290382 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=42{x}^{5}-70{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =42(0)^{5}-70(0)^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{5}(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =7(-1)^{5}(-1-2)=21>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =7(1)^{5}(1-2)=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =7(3)^{5}(3-2)=7\\cdot {3}^{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$” only." "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=42{x}^{5}-70{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =42(0)^{5}-70(0)^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{5}(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =7(-1)^{5}(-1-2)=21>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =7(1)^{5}(1-2)=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =7(3)^{5}(3-2)=7\\cdot {3}^{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186290433 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$”; “The global minimum value is $0$” only.\n\n![](images/53/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $$\n\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},\n$$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$”; “The global minimum value is $0$” only.\n\n![](images/53/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186290437 :question/problem "$\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290438 :question/problem "$\\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290455 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290461 :question/problem "$\\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290504 :question/problem "$\\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290507 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290508 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290509 :question/problem "$\\int z{e}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int z{e}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290514 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x({x}^{2}-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x({x}^{2}-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290518 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290526 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/b8/b81b4602d221eb04aab76b0ed8853ab75a5c3cf93e76cc489344bc15133c4daf.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\n$$" "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/b8/b81b4602d221eb04aab76b0ed8853ab75a5c3cf93e76cc489344bc15133c4daf.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290531 :question/problem "$\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290531 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{5}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={5}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{{5}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={5}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{{5}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290533 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290545 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186290550 :question/problem "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 175921862905