ame{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3 & =3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186292802 :question/problem "Express $z=6\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=6\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$"] [:db/cas 17592186292807 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$" "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\n$$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186292846 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292847 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292847 :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/27/27e33d04bccb0136877dce919662aa5f78bb8315e6b2931461419fc8bc1664bd.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/d9/d98570b1dc97924ccb9af8a252769f0873387b029dcd5f95be8be57942f2fb7a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=2.$\n\n![](images/21/2192d5153baf0170024880585d956aec347caedc0f8ac1aeda56e9a225675863.png)" "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/27/27e33d04bccb0136877dce919662aa5f78bb8315e6b2931461419fc8bc1664bd.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/d9/d98570b1dc97924ccb9af8a252769f0873387b029dcd5f95be8be57942f2fb7a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2.\n$$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=2.$\n\n![](images/21/2192d5153baf0170024880585d956aec347caedc0f8ac1aeda56e9a225675863.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186292855 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 3 & y \\\\ 2 & x & z \\\\ 1 & w & 5\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind the minor ${M}_{12}$ of the element ${a}_{12}.$\n\n${M}_{12}=${{field-1}}" "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 3 & y \\\\ 2 & x & z \\\\ 1 & w & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{12}$ of the element ${a}_{12}.$\n\n${M}_{12}=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186292861 :question/problem "Calculate the product $D{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| $D\\cdot {I}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Calculate the product $D{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $D\\cdot {I}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292862 :question/problem "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{5} & -6 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{5} & -6 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292865 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=2\\cdot -2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,6=2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,2=-1\\cdot -2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-3=-1\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=2\\cdot -2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,6=2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,2=-1\\cdot -2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-3=-1\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292869 :question/problem "Find $Ha$ if\n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$a=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$Ha=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "Find $Ha$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$Ha=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [:db/cas 17592186292869 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\cdot 7+6\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\cdot 7+6\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292870 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & y \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|=$\n {{field-1}}" "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & y \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|=\n$$\n {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186292871 :question/problem "If \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & a \\\\ a & -b & a \\\\ 0 & 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix},$\nfind $Qr.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$Qr=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" "If \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & a \\\\ a & -b & a \\\\ 0 & 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle r=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $Qr.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$Qr=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr>