n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186292888 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292891 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+3y &=1 \\\\ x+4y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+3y &=1 \\\\ x+4y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292921 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$ or $4$), the player wins $$10,$ so we have $p=10.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-2)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(10)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.80.$" "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$ or $4$), the player wins $$10,$ so we have $p=10.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \nP(-2)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(10)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n\n$$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.80.$"] [:db/cas 17592186292934 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}=\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)}.$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3h