{15} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).$" "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x-9 & =0 \\\\ 15x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{15} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186294850 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $q(x)=\\frac{4}{9x-k}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." "If the domain of the function $q(x)=\\frac{4}{9x-k}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ),\n$$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186294851 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{kx+4}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{kx+4}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),\n$$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186294853 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+7}{bx+5}$ is $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{6})\\cup (-\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ),$ then $b=${{field-1}}." "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+7}{bx+5}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{6})\\cup (-\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ),\n$$ then $b=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186294855 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8x-9}{kx-2}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),$ then $k=${{field-1}}." "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8x-9}{kx-2}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),\n$$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [:d