 where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{n}.\n$$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186294897 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186294911 :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-17x & >-2{x}^{2}+45 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=6{x}^{2}-17x-45$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-27x)+(10x-45) & =0 \\\\ 3x(2x-9)+5(2x-9) & =0 \\\\ (3x+5)(2x-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{9}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f73c9906e6b65d1ddbda91619c7ffbb77408faa0541e2bc16565e0127d334312.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-45>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<-\\frac{5}{3}$ or $x>\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})⋃(\\frac{9}{2},\\infty ).$" "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-17x & >-2{x}^{2}+45 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=6{x}^{2}-17x-45$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-27x)+(10x-45) & =0 \\\\ 3x(2x-9)+5(2x-9) & =0 \\\\ (3x+5)(2x-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{9}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f73c9906e6b65d1ddbda91619c7ffbb77408faa0541e2bc16565e0127d334312.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-45>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<-\\frac{5}{3}$ or $x>\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})⋃(\\frac{9}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186294916 :question/problem "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}=1\n$$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186294936 :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186294954 :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & >49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >\\frac{49}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} \\\\ |x| & >\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{6}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},+\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>\\frac{49}{36}$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{6})(x+\\frac{7}{6}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots are  $x=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}$   is positive, the parabola opens upward. Hence, the solution set to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots and does not include them:\n\n![](images/14/14702e7d185d38587cf10642c8a455d9d25a51d2a8eebbf7e0e54a79223ea56e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},\\infty ).\n$$" "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & >49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >\\frac{49}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} \\\\ |x| & >\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{6}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},+\\infty ).\n$$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>\\frac{49}{36}$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{6})(x+\\frac{7}{6}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots are  $x=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}$   is positive, the parabola opens upward. Hence, the solution set to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots and does not include them:\n\n![](images/14/14702e7d185d38587cf10642c8a455d9d25a51d2a8eebbf7e0e54a79223ea56e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186294979 :question/problem "The center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "The center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186294984 :question/problem "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$" "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$"] [:db/cas 17592186294986 :question/problem "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}=1$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}=1\n$$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186295008 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{49}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{7}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{49}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{7}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186295009 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{11}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{11}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186295010 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186295011 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{81}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{81}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186295048 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186295052 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186295058 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$⋃[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$" "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle ⋃[\n$$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$"] [:db/cas 17592186295215 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(2x-1)(1-x)(3x-4)(x-5).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $6$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},1),\\,(1,\\frac{4}{3}),\\,(\\frac{4}{3},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ | $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$ | $(\\frac{4}{3},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)\\le 0$ on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$, $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$, and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]⋃[1,\\frac{4}{3}]⋃[5,\\infty ).$" "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(2x-1)(1-x)(3x-4)(x-5).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $6$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},1),\\,(1,\\frac{4}{3}),\\,(\\frac{4}{3},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ | $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$ | $(\\frac{4}{3},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)\\le 0$ on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$, $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$, and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]⋃[1,\\frac{4}{3}]⋃[5,\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186295216 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(3x-1)^{2}(2x+5)^{2}(4-x)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }2,$ and $4.$ These roots divide the number line into five subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},2),\\,(2,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(3x-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(2x+5)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(4-x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(x-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is non-negative only on the closed interval $(2,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in {-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}}⋃[2,4].$" "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(3x-1)^{2}(2x+5)^{2}(4-x)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }2,\n$$ and $4.$ These roots divide the number line into five subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},2),\\,(2,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(3x-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(2x+5)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(4-x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(x-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is non-negative only on the closed interval $(2,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in {-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}}⋃[2,4].$"] [:db/cas 17592186295244 :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >4{x}^{5}+8{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4} & >0 \\\\ -4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+x+2) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2}(x+2)+(x+2)) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(-4{x}^{2}+1) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-4{x}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $1+2x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)⋃(-\\frac{1}{2},0)⋃(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >4{x}^{5}+8{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4} & >0 \\\\ -4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+x+2) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2}(x+2)+(x+2)) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(-4{x}^{2}+1) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-4{x}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $1+2x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)⋃(-\\frac{1}{2},0)⋃(0,\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297157 :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}=-1+\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=-1-\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}=\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}$ is also a root." "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}=-1+\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}\n$$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $$\n\\displaystyle x=-1-\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}=\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}\n$$ is also a root."] [:db/cas 17592186297168 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $” is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $” is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297169 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=24\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }$ and $r=24.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the ball is decreasing at a rate of $16{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=24\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\n$$ and $r=24.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the ball is decreasing at a rate of $16{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186297239 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297240 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297242 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297243 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297244 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297245 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297246 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297247 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297248 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297250 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297250 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297251 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297256 :question/problem "If $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ then what is $Y?$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},\n$$ then what is $Y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186297256 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} &= \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} \\\\ &= \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 \\\\ &= \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} &= \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} \\\\ &= \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 \\\\ &= \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297278 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297283 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297286 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186297287 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297296 :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297297 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x>-1.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\nfor $x>-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186297298 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants." "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants."] [:db/cas 17592186297312 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297313 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}\\frac{1}{5}x{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}\\frac{1}{5}x{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297314 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297315 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}x{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}x{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297327 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=5$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=5.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=5}=$\n{{field-1}}" "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=5$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=5.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)|}_{x=5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297341 :question/problem "$\\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297342 :question/problem "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297344 :question/problem "$\\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297347 :question/problem "$\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297358 :question/problem "$\\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297372 :question/problem "For the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." "For the curve $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2,\n$$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186297372 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$" "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x},\n$$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186297379 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297381 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297382 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297384 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297385 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297386 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297389 :question/problem "$\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297390 :question/problem "$\\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297395 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297395 :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)" "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}+1.\n$$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186297396 :question/problem "$\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297397 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297400 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int x{5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297407 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$" "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$"] [:db/cas 17592186297410 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297411 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186297427 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186297428 :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n ${\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}($\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$" "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186297432 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186297434 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186297435 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186297436 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n