 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 5 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=(-1)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=5\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186299672 :question/problem "![](images/55/551c38c3bedd56d8fc1c4fef9f1fcc15b9fbf6fa83af81253b7a4d2296aaeb38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/55/551c38c3bedd56d8fc1c4fef9f1fcc15b9fbf6fa83af81253b7a4d2296aaeb38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186299673 :question/problem "![](images/95/95e601203c55e93922554e4eb56a9742f4a84466ac387e3358484baaf9287e08.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "![](images/95/95e601203c55e93922554e4eb56a9742f4a84466ac387e3358484baaf9287e08.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\di