\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$" "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{(1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i},\n$$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}>0$ and $y=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{|y|}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302065 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y &=8 \\\\ -3x+4y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3\\text{ } & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y &=8 \\\\ -3x+4y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3\\text{ } & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302066 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0x+7y &=-14 \\\\ -5x+2y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 2-(-5)\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0x+7y &=-14 \\\\ -5x+2y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 2-(-5)\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{35}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302067 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=3 \\\\ 0x+5y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-y &=3 \\\\ 0x+5y &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302069 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302100 :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9sin\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$" "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9sin\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302105 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{7}{2} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x-\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+\\frac{11}{4} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(y+\\frac{11}{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}=9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302121 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302126 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ if $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302129 :question/problem "Given that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k,$ \n$c=i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k,$\nand \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ what is the value of $u?$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k,\n$$ \n$c=i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k,$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,\n$$ what is the value of $u?$"] [:db/cas 17592186302129 :question/worked-solution "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}+u-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $u=0.$" "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+u\\,j-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}+u-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,\n$$ we must have $u=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186302152 :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302153 :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$" "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302155 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =1((-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+\\frac{9}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{17}{8}.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =1((-1)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+\\frac{9}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{8}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{17}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302162 :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality  $\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)⋃[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality  $\\frac{(x+5)(x-2)}{x+1}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in [$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle )⋃[\n$$\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186302163 :question/problem "Express $z=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$"] [:db/cas 17592186302176 :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=({e}^{-